<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Zaragoza</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Zaragoza is a city in north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ebro-river">Ebro River</a>, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">medieval period</a> the capital of an important <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taifa">taifa</a> kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a>. It was the most important Almoravid city in north-eastern Spain. Its fall to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-i-of-aragon">Alfonso I of Aragon</a> in 1118 marked the deterioration of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almoravid-empire">Almoravids</a>' northern front.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zaragoza.jpg" alt="Zaragoza" width="320" height="156" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Turol Jones, un artista de cojones from Villanueva del Cascajal, República Independiente de Mi Casa · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zaragoza_-_Bas%C3%ADlica_del_Pilar_y_r%C3%ADo_Ebro.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Zaragoza lies on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ebro-river">Ebro</a> river and its tributaries, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huerva">Huerva</a> and the Gállego, roughly in the centre of both Aragon and the Ebro basin. It stands at about 208 metres above sea level in a rather arid depression, with a semi-arid climate and a cold, dry northwest wind in winter called the Cierzo. The site lies near the middle of the hexagon formed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bilbao">Bilbao</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valencia">Valencia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bordeaux">Bordeaux</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulouse">Toulouse</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>A village of the Sedetani, an Iberian tribe, called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salduie">Salduie</a> stood here first. Augustus founded the city as Caesaraugusta between 25 and 11 BCE as a colony for army veterans of the Cantabrian wars. It had a forum, baths and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a> took it in the fifth century CE without significant bloodshed or destruction. After the Umayyad conquest of the eighth century it became capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-march">Upper March of al-Andalus</a>, with the name Saraqusta.</p><p>In 1018, amid the collapse of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">Caliphate</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a>, it became an independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taifa">taifa</a>, first under the Tujibid family and from 1039 under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banu-hud">Banu Hud</a>, whose rulers included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-al-muqtadir">Ahmad al-Muqtadir</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yusuf-al-mutaman-ibn-hud">Yusuf al-Mu'taman ibn Hud</a>. The taifa flourished in the late 11th century. It fell to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almoravid-empire">Almoravids</a> in 1110 and was conquered in 1118, after which it became the political and spiritual centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-aragon">Kingdom of Aragon</a>. Bubonic plague in 1564 reportedly killed about 10,000 of an estimated population of 25-30,000.</p><p>During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peninsular-war">Peninsular War</a> the city withstood two sieges by the Napoleonic army, from June to August 1808 and from December 1808 to February 1809, and surrendered only after some 50,000 defenders had died.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Zaragoza is the capital of the autonomous community of Aragon, with a population of 699,007. The Basílica del Pilar, La Seo Cathedral, built over the city's main mosque, and the Aljafería Palace are its best-known buildings, and several of them belong to the Mudéjar Architecture of Aragon, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>. The Fiestas del Pilar last nine days, with their main day on 12 October.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Zaragoza” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The taifa kingdoms were then absorbed: Granada in 1090, Seville in 1091, Badajoz in 1094 and <b>Zaragoza</b> in 1110.</blockquote><a href="/empire/almoravid-empire">Almoravid Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>When Abd al-Rahman III succeeded his grandfather Abdallah in 912, the emirate was close to disintegration: the rebel Umar ibn Hafsun held the mountains around Bobastro, and Arab, Berber and muwallad lords ruled Seville, Toledo, <b>Zaragoza</b> and other cities almost independently.</blockquote><a href="/empire/caliphate-of-cordoba">Caliphate of Córdoba · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Celtiberians were a group of Celtic-speaking peoples of the eastern Meseta, the high plateau of central Spain, in the upper basins of the Ebro's southern tributaries, the Douro and the Tagus, roughly the modern provinces of Soria, Guadalajara, <b>Zaragoza</b>, Teruel and Cuenca.</blockquote><a href="/empire/celtiberians">Celtiberians · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>When Alfonso I the Battler of Aragon, conqueror of <b>Zaragoza</b> in 1118, died childless in 1134, his brother Ramiro II left the monastery to rule and secure an heir.</blockquote><a href="/empire/crown-of-aragon">Crown of Aragon · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Frontier zones called the Upper, Middle and Lower Marches, centred on <b>Zaragoza</b>, Toledo and Mérida, faced the Christian north and were held by local lords, among whom the Banu Qasi of the Ebro valley grew especially powerful.</blockquote><a href="/empire/emirate-of-cordoba">Emirate of Córdoba · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Mediterranean coast and the Ebro valley had felt Greek, Phoenician and Iberian influence for centuries and became thoroughly Roman, with cities such as Tarraco, Barcino (Barcelona), Emporiae, Valentia, Saguntum, Carthago Nova and <b>Caesaraugusta</b> (Zaragoza).</blockquote><a href="/empire/hispania-tarraconensis">Hispania Tarraconensis · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
